<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/VARRO/de_re_rustica/e/3/10/text/16>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 16 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/VARRO/de_re_rustica/e/3/10> ;
      zoo:text "others hold that they are the descendants of those barnyard fowls which were carried there by sailors and became wild." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/VARRO/de_re_rustica/e/3/10/text/5>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 5 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/VARRO/de_re_rustica/e/3/10> ;
      zoo:text "together; for chicks require twice ten days, and peafowl chicks thrice nine. The hens should be shut up so that they may sit day and night, except at the times morning and evening, when food and drink are being given them." .

<http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/VARRO/de_re_rustica/e/1/7/text/14>
      a       zoo:Paragraph ;
      zoo:identifier 14 ;
      zoo:isPartOf <http://ns.inria.fr/zoomathia/VARRO/de_re_rustica/e/1/7> ;
      zoo:text "Cato, you know, interjected Stolo, in arranging plots according to the degree of existence, formed nine categories: first, land on which vines can bear a large quantity of wine of good quality; second, land suited for a watered garden; third, for an osier bed; fourth, for olives; fifth, for meadows; sixth, for a grain field; seventh, for a wood lot: eighth, for an orchard; ninth, for a mast grove." .

